<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bono State</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1723 CE (approx.) · capital Bono Manso</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Early Akan kingdom at the forest edge in central Ghana, a gold-trading partner of Mande merchants and forerunner of later Akan states.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bono, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bono-manso">Bono Manso</a> near modern Techiman, is regarded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> tradition as one of the earliest Akan kingdoms. It grew where the savanna meets the forest, close to gold-bearing land and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande</a> trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begho">Begho</a>. Tradition dates its founding as early as the 13th century, though its chronology is uncertain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bono's kings controlled gold mining and the trade in gold and kola northward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> traders, receiving cloth, salt and brass. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> institutions often credited to Bono include the use of gold weights, stools as symbols of office and matrilineal succession. Tradition names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ameyaw-kwaakye">Ameyaw Kwaakye</a> as a king under whom gold mining expanded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1723 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opoku-ware-i">Opoku Ware</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bono-manso">Bono Manso</a> and destroyed the town. Its people and royal stools were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> system, and the Techiman traditional state claims succession from Bono.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1450 CE – 1723 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:5.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1723 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bono Manso</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ameyaw Kwaakye</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Akan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bono Twi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bono, Bonoman, Bono-Manso</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=bono-state" aria-label="Bono State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4652 1881 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bono State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4973 2230h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4972 2137h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4972 2137h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ghana. <a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=bono-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

